  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Sainte-Baume massif stretches along the border between the Var and the Bouches-du-Rhône. Listed as a regional nature park, it offers family-friendly trails suited to most levels, in a wooded setting quite different from the coast. The village of Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume (686 m), with its Hostellerie de la Sainte-Baume, is the classic starting point towards the Grotte de Sainte-Marie-Madeleine, a pilgrimage site tucked into the cliff. The massif is also crossed by two long-distance hiking routes, the GR9 and the GR98.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the Sainte-Baume regional nature park, dogs must be kept on a leash in the Sainte-Baume biological reserve and are banned at the grotto. The ministerial order of 24 March 2025, which extended the reserve (488 ha) and converted part of it into a fully protected biological reserve, specifies the rule: dogs on a leash throughout the reserve, and forbidden in plots 32 and 33 of the Vallon de Castelette. Dogs running loose remain forbidden in all cases. Livestock guardian dogs may also be present on some routes: go around the flock and keep your dog close. From June to September, access to the forest massifs is regulated by the Var and Bouches-du-Rhône prefectures because of fire risk (maps published every evening at 6pm): check them before setting off.</p>
      <p>For the grotto, the Park's listing specifies that access is free and that domestic animals are not admitted there and must be kept on a leash in the forest. The sanctuary is open all year, with summer hours of 7:30am to 6:30pm and winter hours of 8:30am to 5pm; from June to September access remains subject to the forest massif restrictions. So plan to leave your dog outside, under the watch of someone if there are several of you, or give up the indoor visit. The sources of the Huveaune, in the Vallon de Castelette at Nans-les-Pins, are separate: the Park and the commune forbid dogs there, even on a leash.</p>
      <p>Other Park rules to know: motorised traffic, wild camping and bivouacking forbidden, mountain bikes and horses forbidden in the reserve (except on the Béguines upper track), drones and picking forbidden. A quiet zone for Bonelli's eagle forbids hiking between 15 February and 15 July in certain areas. As for parking, the main car parks are along the D80 near the hostellerie, at the Trois Chênes car park (overnight parking forbidden from 10pm to 5am from 15 May to 15 October) and at La Brasque; there are no toilets in the forest or at the grotto, and picnic tables are set up at Trois Chênes. The Park reports regular thefts from cars.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: take the chance to stop at Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume, a good stop before or after the walk — and check the day's massif access map in summer before setting out on the trails.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Plan-d'Aups-Sainte-Baume, at the foot of the massif, is handy for a break before getting back on the road.</p>
